ID  - 5593341
AB  - The goal of this experimental study is to compare different education intervention on opioid education for patients undergoing total knee arthroplasty. The specific research questions to address are:
AB  - 1. Does perioperative education pathway reduce opioid refill requests?
AB  - 2. Is education pathway that focuses on pain management provided in-person and via video in repeated sessions more effective than current standard of care education consisting of a single exposure given as part of a broader preoperative presentation covering multiple topics?
AB  - 3. Is there a difference between education provided in-person vs video?
AB  - 4. Does perioperative education improve compliance with multimodal analgesia?
AB  - 5. Does perioperative education improve appropriate opioid storage?
AB  - 6. Does perioperative education improve appropriate opioid disposal?
AB  - Enrolled patients will be assigned at random to one of 3 study groups. Group 1 (control): Patients are referred to the hospital's standard 1-hour virtual patient education webinar prior to surgery.
AB  - Group 2 (in-person): Patients will receive two in-person education sessions (1st session before surgery and 2nd session after surgery). Patients will also receive portable document format (pdf) handouts about opioid and pain management.
AB  - Group 3 (video): Patients will receive two video education sessions (1st session before surgery and 2nd session after surgery). Patients will also receive pdf handouts about opioid and pain management.
AN  - NCT05593341
SF  - ClinicalTrials.gov
ST  - Opioid Education in Total Knee Arthroplasty
TI  - Effect of Perioperative Opioid Education on Outcomes After Total Knee Arthroplasty: A Randomized Study
